                    clock_r4:               `Friday · the steering committee is in the room`,

                    round_name_r1:          `Read the Situation`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Answer the Challenge`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Earn the Veteran`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Make the Call`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the authority situation`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `It is your first Monday leading Helios, the squad that drives <strong>40% of company revenue</strong>. Eleven engineers, including the veteran who was passed over for your job, sit across from you. Engineering teams at Voltaris argue openly, and <strong>4 of the last 6 squad leads promoted from within stepped back or left within a year</strong>. Your director has told you, supportively, that he expects you to <em>establish yourself this week</em>. Before the stand-up, settle three things: where your authority will come from this week, what you most need to learn before you speak, and how openly you will admit what you do not yet know about this team.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Respond to the challenge & choose your stance`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Within the first hour the squad's <strong>most outspoken engineer</strong> challenges you in the stand-up: <em>“You were one of us on Friday — why is your call on the architecture better than ours today?”</em> The room goes quiet. You have roughly a minute to answer, watched by ten engineers deciding how much of the room to extend you. Then choose a stance for the rest of the week, and how much dissent you will actively invite rather than let go quiet.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Earn the veteran's engagement`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The Friday freeze depends on knowledge of the legacy codebase that only the <strong>veteran</strong> holds. She was passed over for your job and has been visibly doing less since the promotion was announced. Decide how you read her withdrawal, how you open the conversation with her, and how concrete a role you put behind that opening. The other ten engineers are watching what you do.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Make the freeze call & set the leadership contract`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `It is Friday. The steering committee and your <strong>skip-level director</strong> are in the room, and a slipped freeze puts an estimated <em>€3.2M of revenue</em> at risk. Decide how you run the architecture decision itself, how you answer the director when he presses you on whether this is real authority or a performance, and whether you say aloud how you mean to make the next hundred decisions like this one.`,

                    r1_source_label:        `In this culture, where does your authority as the new lead actually come from?`,
                    r1_read_label:          `What do you most need to understand before Monday's stand-up?`,
                    r1_humility_label:      `How openly do you admit what you do NOT yet know about your own team? (%)`,
